  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Rolls Royce has $798M in federal contract obligations in FY2026 to date. Rolls Royce won 280 awards this fiscal year to date, with an average award size of $2.8M. Obligations fell 40.8% year over year. The Department of Defense is the dominant buyer, obligating $763M. Aircraft Engine and Engine Parts Manufacturing is the largest market for Rolls Royce, with $723M in obligations, followed by Other Support Activities for Air Transportation at $34.2M and Aircraft Manufacturing at $17.8M. Other federal buyers include the Department of Homeland Security with $34.3M and the National Aeronautics and Space Administration with $430K. Historical annual obligations show multi-year variation: FY21 $1B, FY22 $990M, FY23 $989M, FY24 $860M, and FY25 $1.3B. Current-year totals are fiscal year to date and represent obligations rather than total contract values. Use these figures to compare buying agencies, industry concentration, and year-to-date performance against prior fiscal years.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Who are the main federal buyers of Rolls Royce?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense obligated $763M from Rolls Royce in FY2026 to date. The Department of Homeland Security obligated $34.3M, and the National Aeronautics and Space Administration obligated $430K. These three agencies account for the reported federal obligations in the current fiscal year to date."
      },
      {
        "heading": "What markets and products drive Rolls Royce federal revenue?",
        "body": "Aircraft Engine and Engine Parts Manufacturing generated $723M in obligations for Rolls Royce in FY2026 to date. Other material markets include Other Support Activities for Air Transportation at $34.2M and Aircraft Manufacturing at $17.8M. A range of smaller markets together contributed the remaining reported obligations."
      }
    ]
  },
